 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max has just asked Vika to be his girlfriend, and she has begun to answer. The next few lines are the answer and its happy aftermath. The Russian comes first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это значит да?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Да.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я так рад.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я тоже рада.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Теперь мы вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Да, мы вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это прекрасно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Очень.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я люблю тебя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я люблю тебя, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наконец-то.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я так рада, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's unpack it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это значит да?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Does that mean yes?' Russian says 'this means yes' and lets the voice rise. 'Means' is 'signifies'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Да.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes.' One word. Her answer is as short as his question was long.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я так рад.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I'm so glad.' 'Glad' is a short masculine form, with no ending. The small word 'tak' means 'so'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я тоже рада.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I'm glad too.' This time the word carries 'a', since Vika is a woman. The pair 'rad' and 'rada' are worth remembering.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Теперь мы вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Now we're together.' There's no 'are', just 'now we together'. 'Together' is a handy word.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Да, мы вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, we're together.' Vika repeats Max's words. Repetition is how Russians say yes.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это прекрасно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It's wonderful.' 'Wonderful' ends in 'o', and there's no 'is'. It comes from 'beautiful'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Очень.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Very.' A single word, which stands for 'very wonderful'. Short answers are common in Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я люблю тебя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I love you.' 'I love' is one word with a 'yu' ending. 'You' takes the 'a' form, 'tebya'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я люблю тебя, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I love you, Max.' The same line again, with his name added. A name added at the end is a vocative, and it needs no change.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наконец-то.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'At last.' The word is 'nakonets', with a little 'to' added for emphasis. It's what you say when something has taken too long.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Я так рада, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I'm so glad, Max.' Vika's word for 'glad' finishes with 'a'. 'So' sits before it. Here is the whole conversation again.",
